Workaround

```ts
function _standardToolInputSchema<S extends Schema.ConstraintDecoder<unknown>>(schema: S) {
  const standardSchema = Schema.toStandardJSONSchemaV1(Schema.toStandardSchemaV1(schema));

  return { "~standard": standardSchema["~standard"] };
}
```

Withdrawing my claim here — the layer responsible for this behaviour is being changed right now by a maintainer, so a parallel patch from me would only conflict.

PR #49195 (base `v2`, opened 2026-09-15) rewrites exactly the classification that decides whether this response retries at all:

- HTTP 402 → `QuotaExceeded`;
- OpenCode Zen's typed account-limit codes (`GoUsageLimitError`, `FreeUsageLimitError`, `CreditLimitExceeded`) added to `QUOTA_CODES`;
- the 429 quota phrasing extended with `budget exceeded` / `usage limit`;
- `SERVER_CODES` / `exhausted` / `unavailable` no longer classify a 4xx response as retryable `ProviderInternal`.

The wording in this report ("Free usage exceeded, subscribe to Go") is the free-tier account-limit family that PR adds to `QUOTA_CODES`, and `isRetryable` returns `false` for `QuotaExceeded` (`packages/core/src/session/runner/retry.ts:17-38`) — so once that lands, this response stops entering the retry path at all. Additionally the retry policy itself is bounded, not infinite: `Schedule.max([Schedule.exponential("2 seconds"), Schedule.recurs(4)])` (`retry.ts:49-53`) recurs only while *every* combined schedule still wants to recur, so `recurs(4)` caps the attempts.

Related work, also owned by maintainers: #49030 (retries with no console feedback, assigned) and #49039 (automatic handling of 429, assigned), which cover the remaining observability/backoff asks.

If the loop still reproduces after #49195 lands, the decisive data is the HTTP status plus the error `code` of the response at the moment the loop starts — that identifies which classification bucket it falls into, and whether any client-side gap is left.

